<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
    Legion Control: run one update cycle on this Mac every 15 minutes.

    This is a TEMPLATE, the same way the systemd units on the Linux side are.
    install-mac-agent.sh substitutes the placeholders and writes the result to
    ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.x1f4r.legion-control.update.plist. Editing the
    installed copy by hand is wasted work, the next install overwrites it.

    It is a USER agent, not a daemon in /Library/LaunchDaemons. Everything it
    touches belongs to the logged in user: the agent tree in ~/.legion-control,
    whatever state the services it looks after keep under ~, and any app that
    runs in this login session. A root daemon would need no privileges it does
    not already have and could not talk to the GUI session at all, so nothing
    here ever wants sudo.

    Same division of labour as on every other machine: this job provides a clean
    environment and a cadence, the agent makes every decision. Whether auto
    update is on, whether an update is actually available, and above all whether
    a service is mid work and must be left alone, are all its call.

    Which services exist and what updating one means are the agent config's
    business, not this file's. On this Mac an update can be as small as asking a
    desktop app that has already downloaded its own build to quit and start
    again, so the whole job of the cycle is deciding when that is safe. That is
    precisely the decision the agent's busy rule exists to make, and it fails
    closed: a state it cannot read counts as busy.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>Label</key>
    <string>com.x1f4r.legion-control.update</string>

    <!--
        An absolute node path, resolved at install time and baked in rather than
        assumed. There is no /usr/bin/node on this Mac at all, node comes from
        Homebrew, and a launchd agent starts with a minimal PATH that does not
        include /opt/homebrew/bin, so "node" alone would simply not be found.
    -->
    <key>ProgramArguments</key>
    <array>
        <string>@NODE@</string>
        <string>@LAUNCHER@</string>
        <!--
            `cycle`, not `update`. `update` with no service picks the FIRST one
            and stops, which meant a Mac looking after two things quietly kept
            the second on a stale build for as long as it existed while the
            switch above it said automatic updates were on. `cycle` walks every
            eligible service, records one operation per service under a parent
            record, and lets one deferred service have no effect on the others.
        -->
        <string>cycle</string>
    </array>

    <!--
        Every 15 minutes, the same cadence the timer on the Linux side uses, and
        for the same second reason more than the first. Yes, it means a new build
        gets picked up the same quarter hour. But the cycle refuses to touch a
        busy machine and reports "deferred", so the retry interval IS the
        recovery time: at 15 minutes a machine that finishes its last piece of
        work updates within the quarter hour, at an hour it could sit on a stale
        build all evening for no reason at all.

        launchd counts the interval from load, and it does not stack missed runs
        while the Mac is asleep: it fires once on wake if the interval elapsed,
        which is exactly the behaviour wanted here.
    -->
    <key>StartInterval</key>
    <integer>900</integer>

    <!--
        Deliberately not run at load. Load happens at login, which is the single
        busiest minute this machine has, and an app being quit in the middle of
        the login stampede is the worst possible moment for it. There is nothing
        urgent about an update. Waiting out the first interval costs 15 minutes
        and buys a quiet login.
    -->
    <key>RunAtLoad</key>
    <false/>

    <!--
        The agent prints exactly one JSON object per run on stdout, so this file
        is a readable history of what was decided and when. launchd appends and
        never truncates, but at one line per run it grows by a few kilobytes a
        day, which is not worth rotating.

        It is not the only record any more, and no longer the one that matters:
        every run of the cycle writes an operation record the agent keeps and the
        apps can read back, which is what makes "did that update actually run"
        answerable after the fact rather than a question about a log file.
    -->
    <key>StandardOutPath</key>
    <string>@BASE@/launchd.log</string>
    <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
    <string>@BASE@/launchd.log</string>

    <!--
        Background says to the scheduler that nothing here is ever more important
        than what is on screen: it puts the job in the background QoS class, with
        throttled CPU and disk. The other two are belt and braces on top of that.
        A maintenance job that makes the machine feel slow while it works would
        get turned off, and then it would never run at all.
    -->
    <key>ProcessType</key>
    <string>Background</string>
    <key>LowPriorityIO</key>
    <true/>
    <key>Nice</key>
    <integer>5</integer>

    <key>WorkingDirectory</key>
    <string>@BASE@</string>

    <!--
        launchd hands a job a bare PATH of /usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin. node
        itself is invoked by absolute path above so it does not depend on this,
        but anything the agent shells out to does, and the Homebrew prefix is
        where node's own neighbours live.
    -->
    <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
    <dict>
        <key>PATH</key>
        <string>@NODE_DIR@:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin</string>
    </dict>
</dict>
</plist>
